1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,134
In April 2026, Ryanair sold flights across Europe

2
00:00:04,134 --> 00:00:07,420
from fourteen euros and ninety-nine cents.

3
00:00:07,870 --> 00:00:09,394
Here's the strange part.

4
00:00:09,394 --> 00:00:12,379
By our calculation from Ryanair's own accounts,

5
00:00:12,379 --> 00:00:15,682
flying one passenger costs it about sixty-one euros.

6
00:00:15,682 --> 00:00:19,238
And the average ticket, not the sale price, the average,

7
00:00:19,238 --> 00:00:22,350
brought in less than fifty-six euros this summer.

8
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:25,976
So the ticket doesn't even cover the cost of the flight.

9
00:00:25,976 --> 00:00:27,904
And yet, in those same six months,

10
00:00:27,904 --> 00:00:31,080
Ryanair made two point one five billion euros of profit.

11
00:00:31,530 --> 00:00:32,920
The ticket is the bait.

12
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:34,612
The money is somewhere else.

13
00:00:34,612 --> 00:00:37,816
So let's buy one of those cheap seats, follow it from

14
00:00:37,816 --> 00:00:40,959
the search page to the runway, and keep the receipt.

15
00:00:40,959 --> 00:00:45,250
At every stop, we'll write down what Ryanair earns, and what it spends.

16
00:00:48,850 --> 00:00:53,170
Our trip starts on a search page, with a price that looks too low to be real.

17
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:57,983
Ryanair carries more passengers than any other airline in Europe.

18
00:00:57,983 --> 00:01:02,481
In its last full financial year, to March 2026, it flew two hundred

19
00:01:02,481 --> 00:01:07,180
and eight million people, and filled ninety-four percent of its seats.

20
00:01:07,630 --> 00:01:12,301
That full year includes a winter, a season Ryanair itself describes as loss-making.

21
00:01:12,301 --> 00:01:14,776
So for our receipt, we'll use the full year,

22
00:01:14,776 --> 00:01:17,590
and divide everything by the number of passengers.

23
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:21,640
The average ticket brought in about fifty-one euros.

24
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:24,755
Flying each passenger cost about sixty-three.

25
00:01:24,755 --> 00:01:27,871
On the ticket alone, Ryanair was about twelve

26
00:01:27,871 --> 00:01:30,640
euros short, for every single passenger.

27
00:01:31,090 --> 00:01:35,628
And yet the airline made a profit of more than two billion euros that year.

28
00:01:35,628 --> 00:01:38,290
About eleven euros per passenger, after tax.

29
00:01:38,740 --> 00:01:41,627
So somewhere between the search page and the runway,

30
00:01:41,627 --> 00:01:45,568
every passenger hands over more than twenty euros on top of the ticket.

31
00:01:45,568 --> 00:01:47,900
The first clue is on the very next screen.

32
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:50,672
You've picked your flight.

33
00:01:50,672 --> 00:01:54,016
Before you can pay, Ryanair offers you a few things.

34
00:01:54,016 --> 00:01:54,853
A bigger bag.

35
00:01:54,853 --> 00:01:56,267
A seat of your choice.

36
00:01:56,267 --> 00:01:57,425
Priority boarding.

37
00:01:57,425 --> 00:01:58,840
A rental car, a hotel.

38
00:01:59,290 --> 00:02:02,154
Ryanair calls these "ancillary revenues".

39
00:02:02,154 --> 00:02:06,484
In its last full year, they reached almost five billion euros,

40
00:02:06,484 --> 00:02:09,138
about twenty-four euros per passenger.

41
00:02:09,138 --> 00:02:12,910
That's around a third of everything the airline earns.

42
00:02:12,910 --> 00:02:17,170
This summer, it was close to twenty-five euros per passenger.

43
00:02:17,620 --> 00:02:20,861
And the extras have grown faster than the airline.

44
00:02:20,861 --> 00:02:25,333
In 2019, they brought in a little over seventeen euros per passenger.

45
00:02:25,333 --> 00:02:28,898
Since then, the number of passengers has grown by half.

46
00:02:28,898 --> 00:02:31,620
Revenue from extras has more than doubled.

47
00:02:32,070 --> 00:02:34,709
There's a reason the price is split this way.

48
00:02:34,709 --> 00:02:37,993
The ticket price is what people compare on search sites.

49
00:02:37,993 --> 00:02:40,925
The extras come later, once you've already chosen.

50
00:02:40,925 --> 00:02:43,799
So Ryanair can fight hard on the headline number,

51
00:02:43,799 --> 00:02:45,910
and earn its margin after the click.

52
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:49,469
That only works if you book on Ryanair's own website.

53
00:02:49,469 --> 00:02:53,692
Which is why Ryanair has spent years fighting the online travel agencies

54
00:02:53,692 --> 00:02:57,680
that resell its seats without a deal, sites it has called "pirates".

55
00:02:58,130 --> 00:03:02,997
In December 2025, Italy's competition authority fined Ryanair two hundred and

56
00:03:02,997 --> 00:03:06,600
fifty-six million euros for abusing its dominant position

57
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:10,330
against travel agents, including by blocking their bookings

58
00:03:10,330 --> 00:03:13,554
and asking their customers for facial verification.

59
00:03:13,554 --> 00:03:17,409
Ryanair calls it "legally flawed", paid it in September 2026,

60
00:03:17,409 --> 00:03:20,570
and will get the money back if it wins its appeal.

61
00:03:21,670 --> 00:03:22,042
Paid.

62
00:03:22,042 --> 00:03:23,083
Now, check-in.

63
00:03:23,083 --> 00:03:29,030
And here, some charges seem designed less to be paid than to change what you do.

64
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:34,241
Since November 2025, Ryanair only accepts digital boarding passes.

65
00:03:34,241 --> 00:03:38,353
Forget to check in online, ask for a pass at the airport,

66
00:03:38,353 --> 00:03:43,763
and it costs fifty-five euros per passenger, per flight, in most of Europe.

67
00:03:43,763 --> 00:03:45,278
It used to be twenty.

68
00:03:45,278 --> 00:03:47,297
Almost nobody should pay it.

69
00:03:47,297 --> 00:03:50,832
The point is that everybody checks in on the app:

70
00:03:50,832 --> 00:03:55,160
no counter to staff, and one more chance to sell you a seat.

71
00:03:55,610 --> 00:03:56,894
Then there's the bag.

72
00:03:56,894 --> 00:04:00,319
The cheapest fare includes one small bag under the seat.

73
00:04:00,319 --> 00:04:04,050
Anything bigger costs extra, and regulators are pushing back.

74
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:08,817
In 2024, Spain's consumer ministry fined Ryanair one hundred

75
00:04:08,817 --> 00:04:12,918
and seven million euros over practices it called abusive,

76
00:04:12,918 --> 00:04:17,738
including charging for cabin bags and for printing boarding passes.

77
00:04:17,738 --> 00:04:21,696
Michael O'Leary called the fine "illegal and baseless",

78
00:04:21,696 --> 00:04:26,660
and in June 2025, a Madrid court suspended it while the case went on.

79
00:04:27,110 --> 00:04:32,232
In July 2026, the European Parliament approved new passenger-rights rules.

80
00:04:32,232 --> 00:04:37,908
Prices shown online will have to include a free item of hand luggage, and airlines

81
00:04:37,908 --> 00:04:40,885
won't be allowed to charge families to seat

82
00:04:40,885 --> 00:04:43,723
children under fourteen next to a parent.

83
00:04:43,723 --> 00:04:46,630
The rules are expected to apply from 2027.

84
00:04:47,730 --> 00:04:48,932
Now, the airport.

85
00:04:48,932 --> 00:04:51,690
And it may not be the one you'd expect.

86
00:04:52,140 --> 00:04:55,634
By Ryanair's own comparison, it pays about eight

87
00:04:55,634 --> 00:04:59,492
euros per passenger for airports and ground handling.

88
00:04:59,492 --> 00:05:01,384
easyJet pays twenty-seven.

89
00:05:01,384 --> 00:05:05,388
Part of the reason is where it flies: often to smaller,

90
00:05:05,388 --> 00:05:09,100
regional airports, which compete to get its planes.

91
00:05:09,550 --> 00:05:14,457
For a regional airport, a Ryanair base means tourists and jobs.

92
00:05:14,457 --> 00:05:18,430
So some have offered discounts, and sometimes more:

93
00:05:18,430 --> 00:05:23,649
contracts in which the airport, or local authorities, pay Ryanair's

94
00:05:23,649 --> 00:05:28,790
marketing subsidiary to promote their region on Ryanair's website.

95
00:05:29,240 --> 00:05:33,964
The European Commission has looked at several of these deals.

96
00:05:33,964 --> 00:05:38,147
At Angoulême, in western France, the airport paid that

97
00:05:38,147 --> 00:05:43,258
subsidiary more than nine hundred thousand euros over three years,

98
00:05:43,258 --> 00:05:46,899
under a contract tied to a new route to London.

99
00:05:46,899 --> 00:05:51,158
In 2014, the Commission ruled that Ryanair had received

100
00:05:51,158 --> 00:05:54,721
illegal state aid there, and at Pau and Nîmes.

101
00:05:54,721 --> 00:05:59,368
In 2019, it ordered France to recover about eight and a half

102
00:05:59,368 --> 00:06:02,853
million euros paid to Ryanair at Montpellier,

103
00:06:02,853 --> 00:06:08,120
calling it "an unfair and selective advantage over its competitors".

104
00:06:08,570 --> 00:06:12,853
Ryanair has contested these decisions, and it hasn't lost them all.

105
00:06:12,853 --> 00:06:17,903
In 2008, an EU court annulled the Commission's ruling on Charleroi, in Belgium.

106
00:06:17,903 --> 00:06:21,163
But in November 2018, French authorities grounded a

107
00:06:21,163 --> 00:06:24,807
Ryanair plane at Bordeaux to recover the Angoulême money.

108
00:06:24,807 --> 00:06:29,410
Ryanair paid five hundred and twenty-five thousand euros to get it back.

109
00:06:29,860 --> 00:06:32,723
Today, the game is played between countries.

110
00:06:32,723 --> 00:06:37,862
Ryanair says it's moving planes to places that cut aviation taxes, like Sweden,

111
00:06:37,862 --> 00:06:42,937
Italy, Albania and Morocco, and away from Germany, Austria and regional Spain.

112
00:06:42,937 --> 00:06:46,450
This winter, its base at Charleroi drops from nineteen

113
00:06:46,450 --> 00:06:49,898
planes to twelve, and Vienna from twenty to fourteen.

114
00:06:49,898 --> 00:06:52,500
The planes go where the fees are lowest.

115
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:56,214
Up the stairs and onto the plane.

116
00:06:56,214 --> 00:06:58,591
Almost certainly a Boeing 737.

117
00:06:58,591 --> 00:07:05,009
Of Ryanair's roughly six hundred and fifty aircraft, all but twenty-six are 737s.

118
00:07:05,009 --> 00:07:10,000
One type means one kind of training and one set of spare parts.

119
00:07:10,450 --> 00:07:13,383
How Ryanair buys them matters even more.

120
00:07:13,383 --> 00:07:16,389
Rule one: buy when nobody else is buying.

121
00:07:16,389 --> 00:07:21,227
In January 2002, four months after the September eleventh attacks,

122
00:07:21,227 --> 00:07:26,140
Boeing was having what its own sales chief called a difficult year.

123
00:07:26,140 --> 00:07:30,685
Ryanair ordered one hundred 737s, with options for fifty more,

124
00:07:30,685 --> 00:07:34,864
worth nine point one billion dollars at catalogue prices.

125
00:07:34,864 --> 00:07:38,970
Boeing called its own offer "exceptionally competitive".

126
00:07:39,420 --> 00:07:41,663
Rule two: squeeze in more seats.

127
00:07:41,663 --> 00:07:47,410
The version Ryanair calls the "Gamechanger" has one hundred and ninety-seven seats

128
00:07:47,410 --> 00:07:50,143
instead of one hundred and eighty-nine,

129
00:07:50,143 --> 00:07:53,297
and burns sixteen percent less fuel per seat.

130
00:07:53,297 --> 00:07:57,572
The next one, the bigger 737 MAX 10, was finally certified by

131
00:07:57,572 --> 00:08:02,338
America's aviation regulator in October 2026, after years of delays.

132
00:08:02,338 --> 00:08:06,333
Ryanair expects European approval by the end of the year,

133
00:08:06,333 --> 00:08:08,926
and its first sixteen in spring 2027.

134
00:08:08,926 --> 00:08:12,780
It has ordered up to three hundred, worth forty billion

135
00:08:12,780 --> 00:08:16,986
dollars at list prices, and says each one has twenty percent

136
00:08:16,986 --> 00:08:20,700
more seats and is twenty percent more fuel-efficient.

137
00:08:21,150 --> 00:08:22,712
Rule three: pay cash.

138
00:08:22,712 --> 00:08:27,474
Six hundred and twenty of its Boeings carry no debt or mortgage,

139
00:08:27,474 --> 00:08:30,821
and since repaying its last bond in May 2026,

140
00:08:30,821 --> 00:08:33,946
Ryanair has described itself as debt-free.

141
00:08:33,946 --> 00:08:36,773
No lease payments, almost no interest.

142
00:08:36,773 --> 00:08:39,898
By Ryanair's count, owning and maintaining

143
00:08:39,898 --> 00:08:43,022
its planes costs nine euros per passenger.

144
00:08:43,022 --> 00:08:44,510
At Wizz Air, twenty.

145
00:08:45,610 --> 00:08:47,662
Doors closed, engines on.

146
00:08:47,662 --> 00:08:53,570
And here, our receipt hits the one big cost Ryanair can't squeeze: fuel.

147
00:08:54,020 --> 00:08:58,033
Fuel was about forty-one percent of Ryanair's costs in

148
00:08:58,033 --> 00:09:02,120
its last full year, and forty-four percent this summer.

149
00:09:02,120 --> 00:09:06,578
To protect itself, Ryanair buys most of its fuel in advance.

150
00:09:06,578 --> 00:09:12,820
Until March 2027, eighty percent is locked in at about sixty-seven dollars a barrel.

151
00:09:13,270 --> 00:09:15,637
The other twenty percent is not.

152
00:09:15,637 --> 00:09:20,150
And in 2026, the price of that unhedged fuel roughly doubled.

153
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:23,764
On November second, Ryanair published the result.

154
00:09:23,764 --> 00:09:27,508
Over the summer half, it flew six percent more passengers,

155
00:09:27,508 --> 00:09:30,865
but average fares fell four percent, costs rose ten,

156
00:09:30,865 --> 00:09:35,320
and profit fell fifteen percent, to two point one five billion euros.

157
00:09:35,770 --> 00:09:37,297
And the hedge runs out.

158
00:09:37,297 --> 00:09:40,948
For next year, only a quarter of its fuel is locked in,

159
00:09:40,948 --> 00:09:43,537
at around ninety-five dollars a barrel.

160
00:09:43,537 --> 00:09:48,183
Ryanair estimates its fuel bill could rise by about two billion euros.

161
00:09:48,183 --> 00:09:53,759
To pass that on, it says, fares would need to rise by about ten euros per passenger.

162
00:09:53,759 --> 00:09:57,012
Michael O'Leary's verdict: "There is no way it is

163
00:09:57,012 --> 00:10:00,530
going back down to the sixty-seven dollars a barrel."

164
00:10:00,980 --> 00:10:04,494
Air traffic control charges rose nine percent too.

165
00:10:04,494 --> 00:10:08,007
And Ryanair expects EU environmental taxes to cost

166
00:10:08,007 --> 00:10:11,380
it about one point four billion euros this year.

167
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:15,831
Look around the cabin: a few flight attendants,

168
00:10:15,831 --> 00:10:19,966
two pilots, and another big difference in Ryanair's costs.

169
00:10:19,966 --> 00:10:24,885
By Ryanair's own comparison, its staff cost nine euros per passenger.

170
00:10:24,885 --> 00:10:26,097
easyJet: fifteen.

171
00:10:26,097 --> 00:10:27,237
IAG: fifty-four.

172
00:10:27,237 --> 00:10:31,800
Full planes and quick turnarounds mean more passengers per crew.

173
00:10:32,250 --> 00:10:34,092
It also comes with pressure.

174
00:10:34,092 --> 00:10:37,315
For decades, Ryanair refused to recognize unions.

175
00:10:37,315 --> 00:10:40,866
That changed in December 2017, after a pilot rostering

176
00:10:40,866 --> 00:10:44,681
mistake forced it to cancel about twenty thousand flights.

177
00:10:44,681 --> 00:10:47,575
Facing its first pilot strikes, it agreed to

178
00:10:47,575 --> 00:10:50,930
recognize unions for the first time in its history.

179
00:10:51,380 --> 00:10:54,307
Now put the whole cost machine together.

180
00:10:54,307 --> 00:10:59,942
Take fuel out, since every airline pays roughly the same market price for it,

181
00:10:59,942 --> 00:11:05,430
and here's what it costs to fly one passenger, by Ryanair's own comparison.

182
00:11:05,430 --> 00:11:07,333
Ryanair: thirty-six euros.

183
00:11:07,333 --> 00:11:08,870
Wizz Air: sixty-five.

184
00:11:08,870 --> 00:11:10,040
easyJet: ninety.

185
00:11:10,040 --> 00:11:12,894
Southwest: one hundred and forty-seven.

186
00:11:12,894 --> 00:11:16,480
IAG and Lufthansa: one hundred and seventy-eight.

187
00:11:16,480 --> 00:11:19,700
Air France-KLM: two hundred and fifty-three.

188
00:11:20,150 --> 00:11:22,887
Those airlines don't all do the same thing.

189
00:11:22,887 --> 00:11:25,943
Long-haul flights and business class cost money.

190
00:11:25,943 --> 00:11:29,190
But on short flights in Europe, nobody comes close.

191
00:11:30,290 --> 00:11:31,172
Wheels down.

192
00:11:31,172 --> 00:11:36,465
Let's read the receipt for one passenger, over Ryanair's last full year.

193
00:11:36,465 --> 00:11:38,671
Ticket: about fifty-one euros.

194
00:11:38,671 --> 00:11:40,582
Extras: about twenty-four.

195
00:11:40,582 --> 00:11:43,669
The cost of flying you: about sixty-three.

196
00:11:43,669 --> 00:11:46,610
Left over: about eleven euros of profit.

197
00:11:47,060 --> 00:11:50,458
So, how does Ryanair make money on fifteen-euro flights?

198
00:11:50,458 --> 00:11:52,643
It doesn't, not on the ticket alone.

199
00:11:52,643 --> 00:11:54,402
The ticket gets you on board.

200
00:11:54,402 --> 00:11:59,075
The money comes from the extras you add after you've chosen, from costs that,

201
00:11:59,075 --> 00:12:03,869
by its own count, are the lowest in Europe, from planes bought in bad times and

202
00:12:03,869 --> 00:12:08,420
paid for in cash, and from airports and countries competing for its planes.

203
00:12:08,870 --> 00:12:13,371
That model has made Ryanair one of Europe's most profitable airlines,

204
00:12:13,371 --> 00:12:18,589
on a par with IAG, and far ahead of Wizz Air, which barely broke even last year.

205
00:12:18,589 --> 00:12:23,285
It also makes it a frequent target for regulators, and leaves it exposed

206
00:12:23,285 --> 00:12:27,590
to the one line on the receipt it can't control: the price of oil.

207
00:12:28,040 --> 00:12:32,040
The cheapest seat on the plane is rarely the reason the plane makes money.
